  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Macron bans ‘illegitimate‘ protests in France as he tries to get a grip on unrest",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/macron-ban-illegitimate-demonstrations-france-unrest",
      "description": "<p>French president will decide on concrete ‘next steps’ regarding demonstrations on Friday as part of tough security response to recent rioting</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/fallout-fast-tracking-french-rioting-cases\">Angry lawyers and bewildered defendants: the fallout from fast-tracking French rioting cases</a></p></li></ul><p></p><p>Emmanuel Macron has said “illegitimate” demonstrations will be banned over the coming days as France seeks to get a grip on civil unrest after <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/19/looting-french-cities-continues-second-night\">some of its worst street violence in years</a> erupted on the fringes of cost of living protests at the weekend.</p><p>Speaking after a ​meeting with intelligence chiefs and senior ministers, the French president said on Thursday that demonstrations organised by recognised groups would be allowed to go ahead, although their routes could be changed.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/macron-ban-illegitimate-demonstrations-france-unrest\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-22T15:54:13"
    },
    {
      "title": "Angry lawyers and bewildered defendants: the fallout from fast-tracking French rioting cases",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/fallout-fast-tracking-french-rioting-cases",
      "description": "<p>Politicians have called for ruthless sentencing, but lawyers blame the politicians for the poverty, and anger, behind their clients’ looting</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/oct/22/france-protests-emmanuel-macron-ukraine-energy-attacks-latest-europe-news-updates\">Europe live – latest updates</a></p></li></ul><p>Awa, 18, stood in the dock in the <em>tribunal de </em><em>Paris</em>, a small, mute and bewildered figure seeming unable to understand, let alone answer, the simplest of questions.</p><p>The court was told she had joined young people who looted an Yves Saint Laurent store on Boulevard Saint Germain in Paris’s Left Bank late on Sunday and stripped it bare within minutes.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/22/fallout-fast-tracking-french-rioting-cases\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-22T11:08:40"
    }
